        "body": "Privacy and data protection hiring here is small and split: five postings, four distinct openings, mostly at AI companies, and only one is a technical role rather than a legal one.\n\nHarvey lists the same EMEA counsel title twice, in Dublin and London, both remote and both posted on the same day, so the five postings represent four distinct jobs. OpenAI's opening is a research engineer in privacy, and Figure's is a technical program manager for security and privacy. The remaining role, a privacy, cybersecurity and technology attorney in Grand Rapids, arrives through a staffing firm and is the only posting with a disclosed salary, $240,000 to $325,000 a year.\n\nThe pattern matters because privacy work at AI companies is not only legal review. The OpenAI and Figure roles are engineering and program management, which is where decisions about what data can be collected, retained, and fed into training actually get made. That is a constraint on model development rather than on power or buildings, and nothing in this data speaks to compute or electricity at all.\n\nFive postings over a two-month window cannot show demand, trend, or geography. The underlying sources are aggregators and company career boards, so roles advertised only elsewhere, or filled internally, do not appear. Three postings are marked remote while also naming a city, so the remote flag does not mean location-free. The most recent posting is dated September 10, 2026, and the data was assembled September 15.",
